# Lesson 2b: `eval` — three arms, one β-rule

## The semantics, stated as a match

`eval(env, term)` answers: **"what does this term compute to, given what we know so far?"** One arm per constructor:

**`Var(i)` — "read the note."** The environment is where we recorded what variables mean. Look it up. *Trap to avoid:* indices count from the **innermost** binder (Var 0 = most recent), but `Vec::push` appends at the **end**. So `Var(i)` lives at position `env.len() - 1 - i`. Off-by-one here is the classic bug; the formula is the fix.

**`Lam(body)` — "freeze."** A lambda can't compute further — it doesn't know its argument yet. So it becomes a value by *packaging itself*: capture the current environment, keep the body unevaluated. This is the moment `Closure` stops being mysterious: **a closure is what a lambda evaluates to.**

**`App(f, a)` — "fire."** The only arm that *does* anything — this is β-reduction:

1. evaluate `f` → hopefully a `VLam(closure)`
2. evaluate `a` → some value
3. **run the call**: evaluate the closure's body, in the closure's captured env, *plus one new note*: "Var 0 = the argument"

Look at step 3 and see the substitution that *isn't* there: no tree surgery, no shifting, no renaming. We just pushed one entry onto a vector. **That is β-reduction, environment-style.** This is the insight that makes `Value`/`Closure`/`Env` click: they're the machinery that lets β happen lazily.

## Rust mechanics you'll need

Matching on a `TermRef` (which is `Rc<Term>`) — you can't move out of an `Rc`, so match by reference:

```rust
pub fn eval(env: &Env, term: &TermRef) -> ValueRef {
    match term.as_ref() {              // Rc<Term> -> &Term
        Term::Var(i) => {
            // i: &usize; copy it out with *i
            let idx = env.len() - 1 - *i;
            env[idx].clone()           // clone the Rc — cheap pointer bump
        }
        Term::Lam(body) => { /* freeze: build a VLam with a Closure */ }
        Term::App(f, a) => { /* the β-rule, in 3 steps */ }
    }
}
```

And a helper for step 3 — applying a closure to an argument:

```rust
pub fn apply(closure: &Closure, arg: ValueRef) -> ValueRef {
    let mut env = closure.env.clone();  // clone the Vec (clones Rc pointers — cheap)
    env.push(arg);                      // "Var 0 = arg", appended at the end
    eval(&env, &closure.body)
}
```

Notes: `Vec::clone` here clones the *vector of pointers* (each an `Rc` bump — O(n) but shallow; fine for us). `mut` appears for the first time: `env` starts as a clone, then we push. Everything else stays immutable.

## Your exercise

**`src/eval.rs`** (+ the `lib.rs` line):

1. Write `eval` with the three arms. `Var` is written above; you write `Lam` (freeze) and `App` (fire: eval both, `match` on whether `f` evaluated to a `VLam`, then `apply`).
2. In the `App` arm: if `f` evaluates to `VNeutral`, just `panic!("applying neutral: not yet supported")` — we extend `VNeutral` properly in a later lesson. Panicking on impossible-for-now cases is honest scaffolding.
3. If `Var` lookup is out of range (free variable), also panic for now: `.expect("unbound variable")`. Our tests will only use closed terms; the checker (L3+) will make this case truly impossible.

**Tests** — smoke tests that fire real β-reductions:

```rust
